// The OS-level "give this thread's quantum back to the scheduler" primitive
// `worldRendezvous` needs (#2415).
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. `../gcworldstop.bit`'s spin used to count collector
// iterations only, which is a proxy for wall-clock time SOLELY while the host
// is idle. Under contention a merely-descheduled mutator cannot acknowledge a
// stop inside any number of pure-CPU spins, because a busy loop never gives
// the scheduler an opening to run the thread it is waiting on — the
// collector's own OS thread can burn its entire spin budget without ever
// surrendering a timeslice, which starves the very mutator it needs. Measured
// on `_tests_/stress/gcslotexitdarwin`: 0/25 abandoned on an idle box, 55-62%
// abandoned at concurrency 28, base and branch statistically indistinguishable
// — a coin flip driven by load, not by any leaked slot.
//
// Interspersing a real yield converts the bound from "N iterations of
// computation" into "N opportunities for the OS scheduler to interleave the
// thread we are waiting on", which is what actually scales with contention.
// Giving up remains possible and remains safe either way — ABI.md §5 makes an
// expired rendezvous abandon the collection, and this changes only how often a
// merely-descheduled (not leaked) mutator triggers that path.
//
// SAME OS-SPECIFIC SPLIT EVERY SUCH PRIMITIVE IN THIS RUNTIME NEEDS — see
// ./gcthread.bit's header for the full argument. `syscall` (§11.8) is
// Linux-only, `extern fn` (§11.7) is Darwin's and Windows's only route to the
// kernel/scheduler, and both reject even an uncalled declaration of the wrong
// one, so a single cross-platform file cannot hold both bodies. The body lives
// per OS in `runtime/gc/{linux,darwin,windows}/gcyield.bit`; this file only
// declares the extern and re-exposes it under an ordinary Bit name. `gc` is
// already a `PLATFORM_PAIRS`/`WINDOWS_PAIRS` member (`scripts/g2archive.sh`,
// since #3420's `gcthread.bit` split), and that listing is per-directory, not
// per-file, so a new file inside an existing per-OS `gc` directory needs no
// build-driver change.
extern fn bit_rt_port_gc_world_yield(): int

// Give this thread's remaining scheduling quantum back to the OS. Called from
// `worldRendezvous`'s spin (`./gcworldstop.bit`) between bursts of tight
// checks — never on the fast/uncontended path, where a mutator that acks
// within the first burst never reaches this call at all.
//
// The provider's return value is read by nobody: POSIX documents
// `sched_yield`'s result as "always succeeds" on Linux and Darwin, and
// `SwitchToThread`'s `BOOL` merely reports whether a switch actually
// happened — this call's only job is the ATTEMPT, and there is nothing a
// caller could do differently either way. `@nosplit` to match its only
// caller's requirement (a call to an `extern fn` needs no such marker on the
// callee per SPEC §11.7 — the permission attaches to the call site — but this
// wrapper is an ordinary Bit function, so it needs the attribute itself).
export @nosplit fn worldYield() {
  let _ = bit_rt_port_gc_world_yield()
}
